<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 892</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as τὰ βασιλικά, ta basilika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilika, the imperial laws, was a Byzantine collection of law in sixty books completed around 892 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by order of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>, continuing the work of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> to simplify and adapt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> between 529 and 534. Justinian's code was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, by then obsolete in the empire, and many of its laws no longer applied; the Basilika gave the law in Greek, added newer law such as the Procheiron and the Epanagoge, and carried commentaries (scholia). Its influence was limited to the East, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> it was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> until the present Civil Code.</p></div>
